    "q-143487": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143487",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{108}$ be a random sample of size $n=108$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu =20$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma =90.$ Find an approximation for $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<5).$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th width=\"25%\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$z$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.09\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-2.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0021\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0020\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0019\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-2.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0028\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0027\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0026\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-2.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0038\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0037\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0036\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n\n<th>\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th width=\"25%\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$z$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-1.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0344\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0336\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0329\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-1.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0427\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0418\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0409\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-1.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0526\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0516\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0505\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=108,\\,\\mu =20,\\,\\sigma =90.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(20,\\frac{{90}^{2}}{108}) \\\\  & ∼N(20,75).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<5).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<5) & =P(-5<\\overset{―}{X}<5) \\\\  & =P(\\frac{-5-20}{\\sqrt{75}}<Z<\\frac{5-20}{\\sqrt{75}}) \\\\  & =P(-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}<Z<-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.89<Z<-1.73) \\\\  & =P(Z<-1.73)-P(Z<-2.89) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.73)-Φ(-2.89).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-2.89)=0.0019, \\\\ Φ(-1.73)=0.0418.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<5) & =Φ(-1.73)-Φ(-2.89) \\\\  & =0.0418-0.0019 \\\\  & =0.0399.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
