    "q-143491": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143491",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{126}$ be a random sample of size $n=126$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu =5$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma =84.$ Find an approximation for $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>3.5).$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th width=\"25%\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$z$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-1.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1093\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1075\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1056\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-1.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1292\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1271\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1251\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-1.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1515\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1492\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1469\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n\n<th>\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th width=\"25%\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$z$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4207\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4168\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4129\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4602\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4562\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4522\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5000\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5040\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5080\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=126,\\,\\mu =5,\\,\\sigma =84.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(5,\\frac{{84}^{2}}{126}) \\\\  & ∼N(5,56).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>3.5).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>3.5) & =1-P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<3.5) \\\\  & =1-P(-3.5<\\overset{―}{X}<3.5) \\\\  & =1-P(\\frac{-3.5-5}{\\sqrt{56}}<Z<\\frac{3.5-5}{\\sqrt{56}}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(-1.14<Z<-0.20) \\\\  & =1-(P(Z<-0.20)-P(Z<-1.14)) \\\\  & =1+Φ(-1.14)-Φ(-0.20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.14)=0.1271, \\\\ Φ(-0.20)=0.4207.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>3.5) & =1+Φ(-1.14)-Φ(-0.20) \\\\  & =1+0.1271-0.4207 \\\\  & =0.7064.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
