    "q-143506": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143506",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{55}$ be a random sample of size $n=55$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.11,$ calculate $P(1.6<\\overset{―}{X}<1.7).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ | $\\,0.9788\\,$ | $\\,0.9793\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ | $\\,0.9838\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9868\\,$ | $\\,0.9871\\,$ | $\\,0.9875\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =55,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{5}}{20}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{20} \\\\  & =1.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.11.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(1.6,\\frac{0.11}{55}) \\\\  & ∼N(1.6,0.002).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(1.6<\\overset{―}{X}<1.7).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1.6<\\overset{―}{X}<1.7) & =P(\\frac{1.6-1.6}{\\sqrt{0.002}}<Z<\\frac{1.7-1.6}{\\sqrt{0.002}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0<Z<2.24) \\\\  & =P(Z<2.24)-P(Z<0) \\\\  & =Φ(2.24)-Φ(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(2.24)=0.9875,\n$$\n\nand by the symmetry of the normal distribution, $Φ(0)=0.5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1.6<\\overset{―}{X}<1.7) & =Φ(2.24)-Φ(0) \\\\  & =0.9875-0.5 \\\\  & =0.4875.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
